WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.500
Borrow checking is not only local to a
line of code.

2
00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:03.437
It shapes API design.

3
00:00:03.517 --> 00:00:06.278
A function that accepts a shared reference
says it only needs to read.

4
00:00:06.358 --> 00:00:09.739
A function that accepts a mutable
reference says it needs controlled modification.

5
00:00:09.819 --> 00:00:13.200
A function that accepts an owned value
says responsibility moves into the function.

6
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:14.518
Those choices affect callers.

7
00:00:14.598 --> 00:00:15.835
If a function takes ownership,

8
00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:19.248
the caller cannot keep using the old owner
unless the function returns something or

9
00:00:19.328 --> 00:00:21.138
the value implements a simple copy
behavior.

10
00:00:21.218 --> 00:00:22.122
If a function borrows,

11
00:00:22.202 --> 00:00:24.916
the caller keeps ownership but must
respect the borrow's duration.

12
00:00:24.996 --> 00:00:28.091
If a function mutably borrows, the caller
gives temporary exclusive access.

13
00:00:28.171 --> 00:00:30.171
Structs make these relationships
longer-lived.

14
00:00:30.251 --> 00:00:33.584
A struct with owned fields is usually
straightforward because the struct controls

15
00:00:33.664 --> 00:00:34.711
cleanup for those fields.

16
00:00:34.791 --> 00:00:36.696
A struct with borrowed fields can be
efficient,

17
00:00:36.776 --> 00:00:39.776
but it introduces lifetime relationships
that every caller must satisfy.

18
00:00:39.856 --> 00:00:40.856
That can be appropriate,

19
00:00:40.936 --> 00:00:43.697
but it should be intentional and
documented through the type design.

20
00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:45.729
Good APIs avoid surprising ownership
transfer.

21
00:00:45.809 --> 00:00:48.666
If a function only needs to read a
request, configuration object, path,

22
00:00:48.746 --> 00:00:50.794
or collection, a reference may be the
right choice.

23
00:00:50.874 --> 00:00:53.111
If the function needs to store the value
beyond the call,

24
00:00:53.191 --> 00:00:55.429
spawn work that owns it, or transform it
into a new state,

25
00:00:55.509 --> 00:00:56.842
taking ownership may be clearer.

26
00:00:56.922 --> 00:00:58.684
The borrow checker reinforces API honesty.

27
00:00:58.764 --> 00:01:01.907
It prevents a function from returning
references that cannot remain valid and

28
00:01:01.987 --> 00:01:05.081
prevents callers from mutating values
while a shared borrow is still active.

29
00:01:05.161 --> 00:01:07.637
Clear APIs make caller and callee
responsibilities obvious,

30
00:01:07.717 --> 00:01:09.860
which improves maintainability and
reviewability.
